  "specDocument": "## Rules\n\nR1: If the reading is **strictly less** than `SafetyBandClassifier.LOWER_BOUND`, the method returns the label `\"BELOW\"`.\n\nR2: If the reading is **greater than or equal to** `SafetyBandClassifier.LOWER_BOUND` **and** **less than or equal to** `SafetyBandClassifier.UPPER_BOUND`, the method returns the label `\"NORMAL\"`.\n\nR3: If the reading is **strictly greater** than `SafetyBandClassifier.UPPER_BOUND`, the method returns the label `\"ABOVE\"`.\n\n*All inputs are assumed to be finite `double` values (i.e., not `NaN`, `Infinity`, or `-Infinity`).*  \n\n## Worked Examples\n\n| Rules | Input (reading) | Expected |\n|-------|-----------------|----------|\n| R1    | -12.5           | \"BELOW\" |\n| R2    | 0.0 (exact lower bound) | \"NORMAL\" |\n| R2    | 45.7            | \"NORMAL\" |\n| R2    | 100.0 (exact upper bound) | \"NORMAL\" |\n| R3    | 123.4           | \"ABOVE\" |\n\n## Design\n\n| Type | Role | Template status |\n|------|------|-----------------|\n| SafetyBandClassifier | Holds the two threshold constants and provides the `classify` method that students must implement. | stubbed |\n\n## Public API\n\n### SafetyBandClassifier\n```java\npackage de.tum.cit.aet.exgencb680d7dc36c49b0;\n\npublic class SafetyBandClassifier {\n    public static final double LOWER_BOUND = 0.0;\n    public static final double UPPER_BOUND = 100.0;\n    public SafetyBandClassifier() { /* ... */ }\n    public String classify(double reading); // returns \"BELOW\", \"NORMAL\" or \"ABOVE\"\n}\n```\n\n## Testing Strategy\n\n| ID | Owner type | Observable responsibility | Weight | After due date? |\n|----|------------|---------------------------|--------|-----------------|\n| S1 | SafetyBandClassifier | `classify` returns the correct label for each partition: below lower, exactly lower, between bounds, exactly upper, above upper. | 3 | no |\n\n## Contract Risk Inventory\n\n| Seam | Rules | Admitted partitions | Excluded inputs |\n|------|-------|---------------------|-----------------|\n| S1 | R1, R2, R3 | S1.P1: reading < LOWER_BOUND; S1.P2: reading == LOWER_BOUND; S1.P3: LOWER_BOUND < reading < UPPER_BOUND; S1.P4: reading == UPPER_BOUND; S1.P5: reading > UPPER_BOUND | NaN, +Infinity, -Infinity |\n\n## Decision Ledger\n\n| Decision | Provenance | Why necessary | Observable |\n|----------|------------|----------------|------------|\n| Labels \"BELOW\", \"NORMAL\", \"ABOVE\" | EXPLICIT_BRIEF (derived from \"three bands\") | Gives a concrete, testable output for each band | Returned string value |\n| Equality on both bounds maps to NORMAL | EXPLICIT_BRIEF (\"explicitly defines the result when a reading is exactly on the lower or upper boundary\") | Removes ambiguity for edge cases | Which rule fires for exact bound inputs |\n| API: single class with static constants and `classify` method | NECESSARY_OPERATIONAL_CHOICE (minimal API) | Provides a clear entry point for students without extra scaffolding | Presence of method signature |\n| Domain restriction to finite doubles | INPUT_DOMAIN_ASSUMPTION (to avoid undefined behavior for NaN/Infinity) | Guarantees every admissible input matches a rule | Test inputs exclude NaN/Infinity |\n\n## Diagram\n\nno – the exercise consists of a single class with no collaborating types.\n",
